# Flutter Architecture

This rule is the constraint. The skill `flutter-apply-architecture-best-practices` is the workflow for a new feature, `flutter-wrap-package-service` is the workflow for adding an infrastructure package, and `flutter-build-form` is the workflow for a form. All of them follow this rule. Do not introduce `ChangeNotifier`, `ViewModel`, or `ListenableBuilder` for feature state, or a root layout of `lib/data`, `lib/domain`, and `lib/ui`.

Feature state is a Cubit. Business operations go through a use case. Datasources, repositories, and use cases return `Either` or `TaskEither`. Infrastructure SDKs sit behind core services. Dependencies are constructor-injected with `get_it` and `injectable`. Navigation is GoRouter. Forms use `formz`; add it only when the project gets its first form.

Before editing, read the nearest feature and copy its folders, names, imports, annotations, and result type. If that code contradicts this rule (for example a `Bloc` with events, or an SDK imported in a datasource), new code follows this rule and the old code stays as it is unless the request asks to change it.

Make the smallest change that satisfies the request. Do not refactor unrelated code. Do not add a dependency that duplicates one already in `pubspec.yaml`. Do not hand-edit generated files (`*.g.dart`, `*.freezed.dart`, `*.config.dart`). After the change, run format, `dart analyze`, and the relevant tests when they exist, and say which commands ran.

## Project structure

```text
lib/
├── app/
│   └── router/
├── core/
│   ├── config/
│   ├── di/
│   ├── errors/
│   ├── forms/
│   ├── network/
│   ├── services/
│   └── storage/
└── features/
    └── feature_name/
        ├── data/
        │   ├── datasources/
        │   ├── models/
        │   ├── repositories/
        │   └── requests/
        ├── domain/
        │   ├── entities/
        │   ├── failures/
        │   ├── repositories/
        │   └── use_cases/
        └── presentation/
            ├── cubit/
            ├── inputs/
            ├── pages/
            └── widgets/
```

Create a directory only when a file is about to live there. Do not scaffold empty layers.

- `domain/entities`: business objects. No JSON, no Flutter, no HTTP types.
- `domain/failures`: failures only this feature produces (`EmailNotVerifiedFailure`), extending the core `Failure`. Skip this folder when the feature has none.
- `domain/repositories`: abstract contracts. No implementations.
- `domain/use_cases`: one class per business operation, named `<Operation>UseCase` in `<operation>_use_case.dart` (`SignInUseCase` in `sign_in_use_case.dart`).
- `data/models`: response DTOs and `toEntity()`. JSON stays here.
- `data/requests`: outbound bodies and query objects. Skip this folder when the feature sends no request-specific type.
- `data/datasources`: the feature's remote or local calls through core services. Skip a second datasource when one already covers the source.
- `data/repositories`: implementations of the domain contract.
- `presentation/cubit`: `Cubit<State>` and its state. No event classes.
- `presentation/inputs`: `FormzInput` classes used only by this feature.
- `presentation/pages` and `presentation/widgets`: rendering and user input.
- `app`: the root widget, the bootstrap shared by entry points, and, in `app/router/`, the GoRouter table and its redirects. It is the only code outside a feature that imports feature pages. `core/` never imports a feature.
- `core/config`: `AppConfig` and its per-flavor implementations (base URL, timeouts, public keys), as described in the Flutter Flavors rule.
- `core/di`: the injection setup and the `@module` classes that register SDK instances.
- `core/errors`: app exceptions, the `abstract` (not `sealed`) `Failure` base so features can extend it, the shared `Failure` types, the exception-to-`Failure` helper, and the `Failure`-to-message extension.
- `core/forms/inputs`: `FormzInput` classes used by two or more features (`Email`, `Password`, `Phone`) and their error-message extensions.
- `core/network`: the `NetworkService` interface, its impl, and `SessionEvents`, which reports an expired session. Only the impl knows the HTTP client.
- `core/storage`: storage services, including secure storage and local persistence, and the shared storage keys (`StorageKeys`).
- `core/services`: other platform or vendor capabilities (analytics, permissions, image picking, sharing).

Put code in `core/` only when two features already need it, except core services, which always live in `core/`. Keep everything else inside the feature. Move a feature input to `core/forms/inputs/` when a second feature needs it.

## Infrastructure packages

An infrastructure SDK is a package that does I/O, calls platform channels, or exposes a vendor API: HTTP clients (`http`, `dio`), Firebase, `flutter_secure_storage`, `shared_preferences`, `image_picker`, `share_plus`, `geolocator`, `permission_handler`, and similar.

Import an infrastructure SDK only in the impl of its core service (`*_service_impl.dart`) and in the `@module` under `core/di/` that registers it. Features, Cubits, widgets, and domain depend on the service interface. Never create an SDK instance inline (`Dio()`, `FirebaseAuth.instance`, `ImagePicker()`) anywhere else.

The service interface uses app types only. SDK types (`Dio`, `Response`, `DocumentSnapshot`, `XFile`) and SDK exceptions (`DioException`, `ClientException`, `FirebaseException`) do not cross it. The impl translates SDK errors into app exceptions from `core/errors/`.

Name the service after the capability (`NetworkService`, `SecureStorageService`), not the package, unless sibling services use package names. Expose only the methods the app uses. Reuse an existing service for an SDK; never write a second wrapper.

Architecture and pure-Dart utility packages are imported where their layer allows them: `flutter_bloc` and `go_router` in presentation; `formz` in presentation and `core/forms/`; `envied` only in `core/config/`; `fpdart`, `equatable`, `meta`, and `collection` in any layer; `injectable` annotations on any injectable class; `freezed_annotation` and `json_annotation` where those generators are already used.

## Dependency direction

The call chain is page → Cubit → use case → repository contract → repository implementation → datasource → core service. Datasources, repositories, and use cases return `Either<Failure, T>` or `TaskEither<Failure, T>`, and the Cubit folds that result into its state. A repository method that watches changes (`watchSession`) returns a `Stream` of entities instead and emits only values that were already saved. Code examples for each step live in the skill `flutter-apply-architecture-best-practices`.

Domain imports neither `data/` nor `presentation/`, and it does not import Flutter.

Data implements domain contracts and maps models to entities. It does not import presentation.

Presentation calls a use case, with one exception: a Cubit may call a repository when the operation forwards a single repository call and adds no validation, branching, or mapping. Any business rule belongs in a use case. Presentation never constructs a service, a datasource, or a repository implementation.

## Cubit and UI

`Cubit<State>` is the only feature state. Do not add `Bloc`, event classes, `Provider`, Riverpod, GetX, or `ChangeNotifier` for feature state.

`BlocBuilder`, `BlocListener`, and `BlocSelector` are allowed on a Cubit. Using them does not allow a `Bloc` subclass.

- Expose methods for user actions (`load`, `signIn`, `submit`).
- Emit immutable states. Prefer a `sealed class` with `initial`, `loading`, `loaded`, and `failure` when those phases differ. A form is the exception: it uses one state class with its `FormzInput` fields, `FormzMixin`, and a `FormzSubmissionStatus`, so field values survive loading and failure. Write that class with Freezed when the project uses Freezed, and by hand otherwise. Never use a Freezed union for a form.
- States, entities, and `Failure` types have value equality so Cubit tests can compare them. Use Freezed only when the project already depends on it. Otherwise extend `Equatable`, adding `equatable` when the project has neither, and write `copyWith` by hand. Do not add Freezed to a project that does not use it.
- Handle loading, success, and failure. Ignore a repeated submit while a request is in flight when a double submit would matter.
- After an `await`, return when `isClosed` is true before calling `emit`.
- A page provides its Cubit with `BlocProvider(create: (_) => getIt<XCubit>())` and renders a view widget that receives state and callbacks. Previews and widget tests target the view. In a form, the stateless field widgets are the views, each rebuilt by its own `BlocSelector`.
- Trigger navigation, dialogs, and snackbars from a `BlocListener`, never from `build()`.
- `StatefulWidget` and `setState` are allowed only for UI state that never leaves the widget: focus, animations, and local toggles such as showing a password.
- Form field values live in the Cubit. Use `initialValue` and `onChanged`, not a `TextEditingController`, unless the Cubit must write the text (prefill for editing, clearing, autocomplete).

## Errors

The datasource is the only place that creates a `Failure`. It calls the core service, decodes with `fromJson`, and maps any exception with the project's helper in `core/errors/` (shown as `mapToFailure` in the skills). If no helper exists, create one there once and reuse it. Do not write a mapping function per feature. The helper keeps the error and stack trace of an unexpected exception in `UnexpectedFailure` and reports it through the project's logger or crash reporter when one exists, so programming errors are not hidden.

The repository does not catch exceptions. It converts the model to an entity (`.map((model) => model.toEntity())`) and may combine datasources or cache.

An expired session is handled once, not per screen. When a request that carried a token gets a 401, the `NetworkService` impl reports it on `SessionEvents`, and the auth repository deletes the token and publishes a signed-out session. Pages and Cubits do not sign out on `UnauthorizedFailure`.

Token refresh is opt-in. Add it only when the request asks for it or the project already has it; otherwise a 401 on a request with a token ends the session. When it is requested, follow the token refresh section in `flutter-use-http-package`.

Presentation turns a `Failure` into text with the shared extension in `core/errors/`. A feature matches its own failures first and falls back to that extension. Do not show raw exceptions or `toString()` to the user.

Use the result type of the nearest feature (`Either` or `TaskEither`). Do not throw for an expected failure (network, validation, not found, unauthorized). Handle both sides of the result. Do not swallow a failure: each one ends in a state or a message, or in a decision the code makes on purpose, such as ending the session locally when deleting the token fails. Do not nest `Either` or `TaskEither`. Do not add a second functional error library.

## Dependency injection

Register dependencies with `injectable` and resolve them through the project's `getIt` from `core/di/`. Constructor injection only. Do not resolve `getIt` inside a service, datasource, repository, use case, or Cubit. Never call `GetIt.I.register*` by hand in app code; only an integration test may replace a registration with a fake after the DI setup runs.

Copy the annotation from the nearest registration. When no sample exists, use:

- `@LazySingleton(as: ServiceInterface)` for a core service impl.
- `@lazySingleton` for a core class that wraps no SDK and has one implementation, such as `SessionEvents`.
- `@lazySingleton` for a datasource, with no contract. Add a datasource contract only when there are two implementations (remote and local, or one per flavor with `@Environment`) or sibling datasources already have contracts.
- `@LazySingleton(as: RepositoryContract)` for a repository implementation.
- `@injectable` for use cases and screen Cubits.
- `@lazySingleton` for an app-wide Cubit, such as the session, that the router or several pages read. Provide it once above `MaterialApp.router` with `BlocProvider.value`.
- A `@lazySingleton` getter in a `@module` under `core/di/` for an SDK instance.

Regenerate the injection config with the project's existing `build_runner` command when registrations change. Integration tests call the project's DI setup before `pumpWidget`: `configureDependencies()`, or `configureDependencies('<flavor>')` when the project has flavors. Do not add another DI library.

## Navigation, imports, and safety

Use the project's GoRouter setup. Add routes beside the existing route table in `app/router/`. Do not add another navigation package. A redirect reads app-wide Cubits, not services, and refreshes through a `Listenable` adapter over the Cubit stream; that adapter is the one allowed `ChangeNotifier` because it is not feature state.

Match the import style of the file you are editing. Do not mix a new `package:` import style into a feature that uses relative imports, or the reverse.

Do not create barrel files by default. Add one barrel per feature only when another feature imports it, or follow the project's existing barrel pattern. A feature barrel exports `domain/` and the entry page only; it never exports `data/`.

Do not hardcode secrets, tokens, or passwords. Do not log them. Store sensitive local values through the secure storage service in `core/storage/`. Environment config and flavors follow the Flutter Flavors rule. Do not add an environment system as part of an unrelated change.

## Tests

Use the project's mock library. When the project has none, add `mocktail` and `bloc_test` as dev dependencies, even when a `dart-*` skill suggests `mockito`. Mock the dependency one layer down: the core service for a datasource, the repository contract for a use case, and the use case for a Cubit. Test Cubits with `blocTest`. Register a fallback value in `setUpAll` for each custom argument type used with `any()`.

## Reuse and scope

Keep each business rule, validation, and transformation in one place. Reuse an existing use case, service, helper, widget, input, or `Failure` before writing a new one.

Field validation (required, format, length) lives in a `FormzInput`. Business validation (account state, server-side policy, rules across entities) lives in the use case. Do not repeat either one in a Cubit, a widget, or the other layer.

Do not add a base Cubit, generic repository, shared helper, or option that the current request does not need. When two pieces of code only look alike but change for different reasons, keep them separate rather than forcing a shared abstraction.

## Done

The change is done when the new feature code follows the nearest feature, state is a Cubit, business rules sit in a use case, the datasource creates the `Failure`, infrastructure SDKs are imported only in a core service impl or a DI module, and dependencies come from the constructor. Tests cover the new behavior when it can fail or branch. Generated files were regenerated, not edited. The commands that ran, and any that could not run, are stated in the summary.
